From a99a705dc5f474e28c25669ac6cc974f4d8fa35c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 21:00:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(webhook): stop losing confirmations on retry, and stop
 timing out into a disabled endpoint
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two defects in the Stripe webhook, one of which silently loses paid customers'
confirmations.

1. THE IDEMPOTENCY GUARD WAS A TOMBSTONE, NOT A CLAIM.
   It wrote a 7-day "handled" marker BEFORE doing any work. If the invocation
   then timed out or threw, Stripe's retry hit that marker and was skipped — so
   the confirmation email for a paid order could be lost with no trace anywhere.
   Now: claim for 5 minutes (enough that concurrent deliveries of the same event
   cannot both run), extend to 7 days only after the work succeeds, and RELEASE
   the claim on failure so the retry can redo it. Failures now return 500 so
   Stripe retries, instead of 200 recording a success we did not achieve.

2. THE WORK COULD OUTRUN THE FUNCTION CEILING.
   vercel.json capped the function at 15s while the handler does KV claim →
   ledger read → ledger write → operator mail → customer mail → ledger write on
   a cold Frankfurt lambda. Stripe retries on timeout, and an endpoint that
   times out repeatedly is DISABLED by Stripe — silently. You would find out
   when a customer said the confirmation never arrived.

   The tempting fix, "respond 200 first and do the work after", is wrong on this
   architecture: Lambda may freeze or reclaim the container the instant the
   response is flushed, so work started after it may never run. That trades a
   loud failure for a silent one. Instead the work is BOUNDED — shared/deadline.ts
   gives a budget with headroom, a per-step ceiling so one hung dependency cannot
   eat it, and withDeadlineOr for steps that are nice-to-have. maxDuration 15→60.

   The two emails are independent and now run in parallel; the operator
   notification degrades to a no-op rather than costing a customer their
   confirmation.

   When fulfillment does not complete, the order simply stays at stage "paid",
   whose DERIVED next action already reads "Bestätigung und Datenanforderung
   versenden". A timeout becomes visible work on the ledger instead of an
   invisible gap — no new field needed, which is the sign the stage machine was
   the right shape.

3. FOUND BY FIRING A FORGED SIGNATURE AT A RUNNING SERVER, NOT BY READING TYPES.
   The new error handling keyed on err.name === "StripeSignatureVerificationError".
   Stripe sets `name` to the generic "Error"; the discriminator is `type`. So a
   forged signature returned 500 — telling Stripe to retry a request that can
   never succeed, and releasing the idempotency claim while doing it. Extracted
   as isSignatureVerificationError() with tests that assert the trap
   (expect(real.name).toBe("Error")).

Verified live against dev-kv with real Stripe-signed payloads:
  valid delivery 1  → 200, ledger record created at stage "paid"
  valid delivery 2  → 200, skipped via claim, no duplicate record
  forged signature  → 400 invalid signature
  claim key after success → "done" (was "claimed" during)
  admin worklist → byVerdict {LIKELY_ELIGIBLE: 2}, the verdict→payment join
  next action → "Bestätigung und Datenanforderung versenden", because Resend is
  unconfigured and the stage correctly did not advance

Evidence: pnpm check clean, 169/169 vitest (16 new), pnpm build clean,
smoke 18/18.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
